window.TALK={"who": [], "say": {"europe": "SAY: My name is Robin Seligman. I am the daughter of a Holocaust survivor.\nSAY: My mom, Helen, passed away in 2013, and I'm carrying on her story of surviving the Holocaust.\nSAY: Today you're going to hear her tell it herself. Everything on this screen is hers: her words, her voice, her photographs, and the records that show what happened to her family.\nCLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: This is my mom, Helen, a few years after the war. And this is her village today.\nSAY: First, let's find where her story happens.\nSAY: We are here, in Los Angeles. (The map moves across the world by itself.)\nSAY: Her story starts on the other side of the world, in a small village in Europe.", "birth": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: My mom was born Helen Helman in April 1929, in a small village called Tiszabogdány, in the Carpathian Mountains.\nSAY: When she was born it was in Czechoslovakia, a country that today is the Czech Republic and Slovakia.\nSAY: Listen to how she describes it. Watch the borders change while she talks.\nCLICK: Helen plays (30 sec)\nSAY: Her village never moved. The countries around it did. Today it's in Ukraine.\nSAY: Has anyone here ever seen Fiddler on the Roof? OK, a few. If you haven't, I recommend it. That's the kind of village she grew up in.\nSAY: And this is her birth record from 1929. There's her name: Helena.", "home": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: My mom lived in a wooden house like this one. Her parents, her brothers and sisters, and nearby, her grandmother Feige, her father's mom.\nSAY: Her first language was Yiddish, which mixes German and Hebrew.\nSAY: They were very, very poor. No running water, no electricity. Kerosene lamps for light. A wood stove for cooking and heat. An outhouse outside. Water from a well. They washed their clothes in the creek.\nSAY: Her mom cooked all day long, everything from scratch. She said she never knew matzah came in a box. What's a box?\nSAY: School was one room. She was a happy girl in a big, loving family, and she didn't know she was poor.\nSAY: In the winter they went sledding, and in the summer they swam in the river.", "balagula": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Her father had a job you won't find anymore. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (23 sec)\nSAY: This is the village road today. In my mom's time it wasn't paved, it was dirt, but it looked much the same.\nSAY: He would drive his wagon to the train station, pick up supplies, and deliver them.\nSAY: When he had more than he could handle, he hired his nephews, who drove wagons too, and they were very strong.\nSAY: Remember those nephews. You'll see two of them again.\nCLICK: Helen plays again: her father hired his nephews. They were all balagulas too. His sister had 14 children.", "school": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Hitler came to power in Germany in 1933, and things started getting very bad for Jews.\nSAY: When my mom was about 7 or 8, she had a teacher who hated Jews. Schools taught that Jews were an inferior race and the cause of the country's problems. That's called scapegoating.\nSAY: My mom was called up to the chalkboard. She froze. The teacher hit her so hard she hurt her own hand, and said: \"If you don't like it here, why don't you go to Palestine?\" My mom just cried, and wished she was in Palestine.\nSAY: In 1939, Hungary took over her region. You can see the border move on the map. Jewish children could no longer go to school, and they were not allowed to speak Yiddish, only Hungarian.\nSAY: A young rabbi came to the house to teach my mom and her brothers and sisters, even though he was scared, because he believed every Jewish child should have an education. One day he never showed up. He was never heard from again.\nPOINT: And this is her village, Tiszabogdány, in the winter of 1939. A street with an inn and a shop, men and boys out in the snow.", "family39": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Her father was Zacharias. Her mother was Roza.\nSAY: Her big sister Hinda. Then my mom. Then her brothers Izak and Majer, her little sister Adela, and the baby, Yiddel. Another baby sister, Cecilie, died in 1940.\nSAY: This photograph is from 1939. The woman in the headscarf is her grandmother Feige, with my mom's aunt, uncle and cousins. It is the only photograph of that family that survived the war.\nSAY: And these are the birth records of all seven children.", "david": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Her father's younger brother David was a butcher. He never married. In 1939 he crossed the mountains into Russia with a few good friends, to survive. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (37 sec)\nSAY: My mom died not knowing. Through my daughter's research, we found out.\nSAY: The Soviets arrested him and sentenced him to three years in prison. Then he fought in the Red Army, and he fell at Stalingrad, one of the bloodiest battles of the war and a turning point.", "gypsy": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Every summer, Roma families camped across the road from her house, right in front of the Tisza River. My mom called them Gypsies, the word people used then. Today we say Roma. The Nazis persecuted the Roma too.\nPOINT: This is a Roma camp from those years, in another part of the mountains. Tents, wagons, horses.\nSAY: She loved watching their camp. When she was about twelve, a Roma girl came over to play with her. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (65 sec)\nSAY: She never forgot that girl.", "adela": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: One day my mom was playing with her little sister Adela, who was about five, when some teenage boys started chasing them.\nSAY: They ran as fast as they could. My mom got away. They caught her sister, and they shaved off all her hair, because she was \"a dirty Jew.\"\nSAY: Adela came home crying and crying. Her mom took out her wig, the one she wore only on special occasions, because religious married women always kept their hair covered, and she put it on Adela.\nSAY: She said: \"Now you are a big girl, like me.\"\nSAY: Adela was so happy in that wig. She walked so proudly. \"I'm a big girl.\"", "horses": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: When the war started, the army came to the house. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (43 sec)\nSAY: For a balagula, the horses were everything. Without them he couldn't work. They took his whole livelihood.\nPOINT: This photograph is from her village, in those years. We don't know whose horses these are.", "keleti": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: In late 1943 my mom needed her tonsils out, and she and her mom took the train to Budapest. It was the first time she had ever been in a big city.\nSAY: Afterward her mom said: stay here with your great-aunt and her family. She lost her husband, and she needs your support.\nSAY: Then her mother got on the train to go home.\nCLICK: Helen plays (49 sec)\nPOINT: pause", "kiraly": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Now my mom was alone in Budapest, with her great-aunt's family.\nSAY: In March 1944, Germany took over Hungary. Jews had to wear a yellow star, and they were forced to live in certain buildings. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (18 sec)\nSAY: This is that building today. And this record, from the Dachau camp, belongs to her great-aunt's daughter. Look at the address: Király 6. Same house.\nSAY: Food was scarce, and Jews were allowed on the street only at certain hours.\nSAY: One day my mom went to buy bread. The line was so long it took two hours. She stayed in line without her yellow star, and she got home after curfew. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (27 sec)\nSAY: \"I was meant to live.\"", "family": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: My mom stayed in Budapest. She kept writing letters home to her family. Listen to what happened.\nCLICK: Helen plays (10 sec)\nSAY: Here is what she didn't know. In April and May 1944, her whole family was forced out of the village, into a ghetto in Mátészalka, and then onto trains to Auschwitz.\nSAY: Her mother Roza. Her sister Hinda. Her brothers Izak, Majer and Yiddel. Her little sister Adela. Her grandmother Feige.\nSAY: They were murdered when they arrived. Only her father, Zacharias, was chosen for work.\nSAY: She would not find out for more than a year.\nPOINT: These photographs were taken at Auschwitz in May 1944: Jews from her region getting off the train, and the selection into two lines.", "buda": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: In the fall of 1944 it got even more dangerous for Jews in Budapest.\nSAY: My mom was hidden in a Red Cross children's home on the Buda side of the river, with about a hundred children. She was one of the older girls, so she took care of the little ones, the toddlers. The little ones called her Mommy.\nSAY: When soldiers found the house, the children ran and hid, and the toddlers followed my mom.\nSAY: In February 1945, the Russian army freed Buda. The war in Budapest was over.", "budapest45": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: These are prisoner photographs from the Buchenwald camp in Germany, all taken on June 2, 1944, one right after another.\nSAY: Her cousin Lajos. Her cousin Michal. And her father, my grandfather, Zacharias.\nSAY: The three were sent there together from Auschwitz, to do slave labor.\nSAY: In 2019 I found my grandfather's prisoner card. It was the first photograph I had ever seen of him, or of anyone in my mom's family who was murdered. My mom never saw it.\nSAY: Lajos and Michal survived. My grandfather was sent back to Auschwitz in October 1944 and killed there.\nSAY: After the war, survivors came back to Budapest looking for anyone who was left. One day my mom ran into two young men from her village. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (39 sec)\nSAY: Her cousins Lajos and Michal, the same nephews who used to help her father with his wagon, came back through Budapest in June 1945. Another cousin, Mordechai, about seventeen, also came through Budapest. We don't know for sure which cousin told her.\nSAY: He also told her about her big sister. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (43 sec)\nSAY: Hinda was seventeen.\nPOINT: Landsmen means people from your hometown.", "names": "SAY: I want you to know their names.\nSAY: Her mother, Roza. Her father, Zacharias. Hinda, seventeen. Izak, twelve. Majer, ten. Adela, seven. Yiddel, four. And her grandmother, Feige.\nSAY: My mom was fifteen. She was the only one left.\nSAY: Years later, my mom was watching a documentary on TV and saw the very place from her dream. She called me. Listen.\nCLICK: Helen plays (10 sec)\nPOINT: pause, then CLICK", "indersdorf": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: After the war, Jewish organizations gathered up children who had no one left. Listen to how my mom left Budapest.\nCLICK: Helen plays (37 sec)\nSAY: Her best friend, Ibi. They met on that truck, and they were friends for the rest of their lives.\nSAY: They ended up in Germany, at a children's center for Jewish orphans called Kloster Indersdorf, run by the United Nations.\nSAY: A social worker named Greta Fischer looked after my mom there, and later helped her get to America.\nSAY: This photo of my mom was taken there in 1946.\nSAY: When she registered there, she used her mother's last name, Landau. And she made herself two years younger, so she would still count as a child. It worked. She kept that birthday for the rest of her life.", "kibbutz": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: The older children were organized into groups, getting ready to start a new life together on a kibbutz, a farming community in what was then Palestine and is now Israel.\nSAY: This is my mom's kibbutz group, in November 1946. There's my mom. And there's Ibi.\nSAY: My mom once told me there were pictures of her in a museum in \"Bulgaria.\" This year my daughter found them, in Bavaria, a part of Germany. We had heard one word wrong.\nSAY: The historians had her name with a question mark: \"Helen?\" I knew it was my mother the moment I saw it.", "prien": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: When her group left for Palestine, my mom and Ibi stayed behind. Ibi's older sister was moving to America, so Ibi chose America. My mom had no family left, so she went with her.\nSAY: They moved to another children's center, on a lake called Chiemsee. This is her card from there. A displaced person, a DP, was someone the war had left with no home to go back to.\nSAY: Then Ibi got sick, and she had to stay behind for a while. My mom sailed without her.", "prien2": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: These are Jewish children at another hotel in town. And this is the lake.", "bremen": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: In April 1947 my mom sailed from Bremen, Germany, on a ship called the Marine Marlin, with other orphans.\nPOINT: This is a ship of the same kind as hers, built as a troop ship in the war.\nSAY: And this is the ship's passenger list, with her name.\nSAY: The list says she was sixteen. She was really eighteen.\nSAY: She landed in New York on May 7, 1947.", "america": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: From New York she went to Chicago. A Jewish children's agency placed her first in an orphanage, and then with a foster family. She stayed close to that family her whole life.\nSAY: [Robin: the banana story. She had heard about bananas but had never seen one, and she didn't know you had to peel it.]\nSAY: [Robin: the orphanage story.]\nSAY: Listen to what she decided.\nCLICK: Helen plays (13 sec)", "la": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: She got married and moved to Los Angeles.\nSAY: I grew up near Third and Fairfax, by the Farmers Market, where The Grove is now. A few blocks from where you're sitting. I went to Bancroft Junior High and Fairfax High School.\nSAY: All of my mom's close friends were survivors, and I thought that was normal. This is my mom with Ibi, the girl from the truck, and Zahava, another friend from the children's center in Germany.\nSAY: Later she married Tibi Katz, also a survivor, who became my stepfather and adopted me.", "visit2005": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: In July 2005, my husband Hyatt and I went to my mom's village. Her house had been rebuilt. The person living there told me that people in the village still remembered where the Jewish families had lived.\nSAY: We found the old Jewish cemetery on a hillside across the river. No sign, just a wooden fence and some leaning stones.\nSAY: Hyatt reported it to JewishGen, a Jewish family-history website, so it would be on record. This is his report. We couldn't read the Hebrew on the stones.\nSAY: This year we learned that one of them is my great-grandfather, my mom's grandfather, Iczik Helman. He died in December 1929, when my mom was a baby. Her baby sister Cecilie, who died in 1940 when she was two, is buried there too. Hyatt's report mentions her. They are the only ones of her family with graves there.\nPOINT: The report says my mom may be the only survivor from the village. That's what we thought in 2005. Today we know her cousins Lajos and Michal survived too.", "grandkids": "CLICK: the photos and records come up\nSAY: Her parents and the brothers and sisters who were murdered have no graves. So she gave their names to her children.\nSAY: She named me Robin, after her mother, Roza. And she named my brother Zachary, after her father, Zacharias.\nSAY: She had three grandchildren, and she loved them very much. When she retired, she helped me raise my kids.\nSAY: This is my mom and Tibi in 2010 with my daughters, Samantha and Rhea.\nSAY: And this is my son Adam, this September, at Kloster Indersdorf, where my mom lived after the war. He is pointing to a photo of her group from 1946. It hangs in the museum there today.\nSAY: My mom passed away in 2013, here in Los Angeles."}, "here": "SAY: This is me as a teenager, about your age, with my mom and Tibi, on Colgate Avenue in Los Angeles.\nSAY: Thank you. (Questions.)"};
